@dsh-external/dsh-policy-strict-gate README · The problem this solves：There is a second reason, and it is the one that convinced me to build this: **a
check whose output lands in a tool result can be skimmed. A check whose refusal
blocks the next write cannot.**

@dsh-external/dsh-policy-strict-gate README · Install：The plugin is installed as a package into a dsh **profile**. `dsh plugin` forwards
to `pnpm` inside the profile directory, so any spec pnpm accepts works.

```bash

@dsh-external/dsh-policy-strict-gate README · Enable the critical-path gate：Ask `strict_gate_status action=targets path=<a file>` to confirm a path really is
protected and by which rule — a glob that silently fails to match is the most
likely way this gate ends up doing nothing while looking enabled.

@dsh-external/dsh-policy-strict-gate README · Development：```bash
npm test        # 72 tests; the integration ones run the real Lean kernel
```

@dsh-external/dsh-policy-strict-gate README · Delivery, precisely：A denial has no `additionalContexts`, so anything queued for that step is appended
to the reason string instead — otherwise a refusal would silently swallow a repeat
notice produced by the very loop the refusal is part of.
